== 4. Tychē Points
#columns(2, gutter: 6mm)[
#strong[Spend 1 Tychē Point to:]

- #strong[Invoke] --- reroll all dice, or add +2 after rolling. This works with any tag or Truth you can justify, even one belonging to the scene or an enemy. Invokes are not limited by the tag cap.
- #strong[Declare] --- establish a minor fact about the scene. The GM may require it to tie to one of your tags.
- #strong[Resist harder] --- reduce one Resistance cost to 0 (§6).

#strong[Earn Tychē Points by:]

- #strong[Compels] --- the GM or another player offers a meaningful complication based on any character tag (power, weakness, or Scar), any of your World, Other, or Self Beliefs (§3.5), or your Instinct. Accept → +1 Tychē Point. Refuse → pay 1 Tychē Point.
- #strong[Compel yourself] --- propose a compel on one of your own tags, one of your Beliefs, or your Instinct. If the GM agrees it meets the rules below, take the complication and gain +1 Tychē Point. Facing it can also earn XP (§12.1).

#strong[Compels]

- #strong[Meaningful.] The complication must change the situation: an obstacle, obligation, exposure, hard choice, or lost opportunity. Flavour or a minor inconvenience doesn't count.
- #strong[Clear offer.] Before you accept, know which tag, Belief, or Instinct justifies it, what will happen, and what it commits your character to. You otherwise control how your character responds.
- #strong[Limits.] A compel must follow from the established meaning of the relevant tag, Belief, or Instinct. It adds no numerical penalty, can't cancel a success already resolved, and an existing penalty doesn't earn Tychē just because a tag, Belief, or Instinct explains it.
- #emph[Feared duellist:] a rival challenges you before an audience whose support you need. #emph[Devoted healer:] you stay to treat a dying enemy and lose the chance to pursue their commander.

==== Beliefs and Compels
All three Belief categories use the same compel procedure. Merely mentioning a Belief or acting in accordance with it does not earn Tychē; the complication must meet the standard above. Several Beliefs supporting one complication still produce only one compel payment. Beliefs do not grant free modifiers and are not additional power tags.

#emph[Belief example:] your Self Belief rejects sacrificing people for advantage. You propose staying to escort stranded civilians instead of pursuing the fleeing saboteur. Before acceptance, agree that the delay will let the saboteur escape the immediate pursuit. Accepting that meaningful complication earns 1 Tychē Point; it does not dictate every action you take afterward.

Declining a compel uses the normal refusal rule above; simply acting against a Belief is not itself a compel refusal or an automatic penalty (§12.3). An Other Belief never grants control over another PC.

=== 4.1 Refresh
- #strong[Base Refresh is 3.] At the start of each session, your Tychē Points reset to your Refresh (if you have more, keep them, up to the cap).
- #strong[Tychē cap.] You can hold at most #strong[Refresh + 3] Tychē Points. Any you would gain above the cap are lost.
- At creation, your first #strong[playbook ability] has no additional-slot cost, and your #strong[Tier 1 Focus ability] is free. If your first playbook ability is #strong[★ Major], pay its 1-Refresh Major cost.
- You may take #strong[up to 2 additional playbook abilities at creation]. Each ordinary extra costs 1 Refresh. Each #strong[★ Major extra costs 2 Refresh total]: 1 for the additional ability and 1 for Major.
- A #strong[★ Major ability] costs #strong[1 Refresh] whenever taken, including through advancement. Add any applicable creation-extra or veteran surcharge, but count the Major cost only once. All purchases must leave you at or above the minimum Refresh of 1.
- An #strong[attuned artifact] (Module A) costs 1 Refresh while you keep it.
- #strong[Minimum Refresh is 1.] You can never trade away your last Tychē Point.
- The advancement option "+1 Refresh" (§12) lets you buy Refresh back.

Design intent: power comes at the cost of narrative flexibility. A low-Refresh character is mechanically strong but has to accept compels to stay fuelled.
]
